# In HTTP, idempotent means:
 Making the same request multiple times should have the same effect as making it once.

# Idempotent HTTP Methods

GET → Fetching a resource does not change anything.
HEAD → Same as GET but only headers.
PUT → Replaces the resource. Doing it multiple times leaves the resource the same.
DELETE → Deleting the same resource multiple times has no extra effect (still deleted).
OPTIONS → Just asks about communication options, no state change.


# Non-Idempotent Methods

POST → Each POST usually creates a new resource. Multiple POSTs create duplicates.
PATCH → May not be idempotent depending on the patch (increment vs overwrite).




